ines that the  president put out saying look America  it's go time  so do you think the president is being  overly optimistic about the  anti-malarial drug and REM disavow which  have seen some positive results in some  trials well I wasn't at today's press  conference were practicing social  distancing but I did see it I was quite  frankly fascinated at these stories that  came out of that  because there was no there was no  disconnect between what Tony felt she  was saying and what the president was  saying the president was saying number  one we have two drugs out there and he  is optimistic and hopeful based on some  of the studies and what they've heard  that these drugs will be helpful to  people and it's why we're providing them  for four people on a compassionate use  basis but Tony Falchi appropriately said  we need to make sure we're following up  the data that we see the studies so that  we can actually tell people for certain  down the road whether this is effective  or not so those two aren't incongruent  that science we're hopeful but we're  also people when they want I'm sorry  what do they tell people you tell people  when they say you know when will these  be sort of in common use when we can and  when anyone who is you know in the  hospital with breathing trouble would be  given these drugs right away what's the  time frame well that's a fantastic  question and we've always told people we  have kind of a three tiered response and  I'll go backwards tier 3 is vaccines we  know it will likely be next year before  we get a vaccine for this disease tier 2  is actually looking at therapeutics  things like rim def severe like like the  drugs that you heard about today and  we've always said it will probably be  mid summer before we have enough data to  be able to say for certain whether or  not these drugs are promising they're  now being made available in record  timing on a compassionate use basis just  in case but Tier one is actually  mitigation and 